    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2026 Paris Motor Show: Jeep Reveals Six-Model European Strategy for 2030, Renegade Is Set to Return\nFiat Ducato, Opel Movano, Citroen Jumper Diesel Vans Recalled Over Fuel Leak Risk\n10 photos\nPhoto: Citroen\nPicture yourself in the shoes of a fleet manager. One of the worst-case scenarios in this role is a van going down mid-route. Fiat, Opel, and Citroen just handed fleet operators a good reason to book workshop time, with Stellantis identifying an increased risk of fire due to a potential fuel leak. Per three distinct KBA filings, the fuel pressure damper in the low-pressure system can leak without warning.\nThe German watchdog lists three reference numbers for this recall, specifically 17165R for the Fiat Ducato, 17166R for the Opel Movano, and 17167R for the Citroen Jumper. All three reports describe the same defect, which is hardly a surprise given that all three models feature similar underpinnings.\nFiat's share is the largest, covering 2,783 large vans produced between July 14, 2026, and July 31, 2026. The Movano recall covers 655 units, while Citroen is aware of 1,486 potentially affected workhorses. Together they add up to 4,924 vehicles, all 2026 models. Production windows differ slightly, of course.\nMost large vans from Stellantis' European subsidiary are produced at the Sevel plant in Italy. Said light commercial vehicle plant builds the Ducato, Movano, and Jumper on a shared architecture that also underpins the Vauxhall-branded Movano, Peugeot Boxer, and the Toyota Proace Max. The Sevel plant opened in 1981 and currently prides itself on a daily capacity of 1,200 vehicles per day.\nCertain versions hail from Gliwice in Poland, which builds select body lengths, wheelbases, and specialized variants across the recalled nameplates. Germany's Federal Motor Transport Authority hasn't clarified where the recall population was produced.\nPhoto: Citroen The Kraftfahrt-Bundesamt lists all three campaigns as monitored, which means that it's tracking completion rather than investigating the reported issue. In Germany, the recall population includes 1,271 units of the Ducato, 225 of the Movano, and 451 of the Jumper.\nThese are all work vehicles, and for a fleet operator, pulling vans off the road for a fuel line replacement costs a lot of money in lost job sites or deliveries. Typical of the KBA, the authority does not say how long the repair takes or whether Stellantis Europe will provide loaners.\nCustomers can check whether their vans are affected by contacting customer support or the dealer of their liking with the relevant campaign number and vehicle identification number. Neither Stellantis Europe nor the KBA has explained why the fuel pressure damper may leak diesel.\nThe fuel tank of said vehicles is likely connected to the familiar 2.2-liter turbo diesel four-pot mill that Fiat markets as the MultiJet III. Said engine shouldn't be confused with the older MultiJet II design or the even older Ford-PSA lump.\nThe KBA material fails to mention whether the recalled vans use the 120-, 140-, 160-, or 180-ps version of the MultiJet III engine. Nevertheless, it does highlight a replacement lower fuel line section, damper included, as the remedy.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nfiat ducato Fiat recall Opel Movano Opel van Citroen Jumper Citroen\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Shelby GT350 Just Sold for Hilariously Cheap – But There’s a Catch",
